  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 126 PM EDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nHigh pressure from Quebec through the western Great Lakes continues \nto promote fall-like conditions this afternoon, while plenty of \nclouds stream in broad low pressure over the Central Plains. Light \nradar returns have failed to reach the ground due to significant dry \nair below 16,000 feet.\n\nNo significant weather concerns through the weekend and even the \nstart of next week. High pressure becomes squeezed by a Nor'easter \n(to the east) and deepening low pressure spilling inland (this \nafternoon) to the Pacific Northwest. By Monday, perhaps some high \nclouds from the Nor'easter spread over northwest Ohio, but no other \nimpacts are expected. Temperatures will warm slightly through the \nweekend, into the 70s, as upper-level heights risen somewhat due to \na buckling of the jet stream to both the west and east. \n\nOur next chance for rain arrives early Wednesday morning as low \npressure moving over the Northern Plains spreads a cold front \nthrough our area. Depending on the timing of the front and \ninstability, would not be entirely surprised to see an isolated \nstrong storm with a 45-knot 500-mb jet in the vicinity, 35-40 knots \nof shear and dew points in the low-60s. Additional cyclogenesis is \ndepicted over the Central US thereafter, perhaps bringing wet-\nweather locally by next weekend.",
